---
import Full from '../layouts/Full.astro';
import Entry from '../components/Entry.astro';
import octicons from '@primer/octicons';

const icon = (name: keyof typeof octicons, size = 16) =>
	octicons[name].toSVG({ width: size, height: size });
---

<Full
	title="Games — Avior Studio"
	description="termcade, a terminal arcade you can run today, plus three Godot projects in progress: Castle Drop, Prizm and Fields of Revik."
>
	<main>
		<section class="page-hero">
			<p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Games</p>
			<h1>One you can play, <em>three on the way.</em></h1>
			<p class="lede">
				A terminal arcade that is public and installable today, and three Godot projects at
				different distances from done. The three are private repositories for now — what follows is
				what they are, not a release date.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="entries">
			<Entry
				name="termcade"
				tagline="A terminal arcade"
				status="Playable now"
				available={true}
				meta="Go · Bubble Tea · wazero"
				command="go run github.com/aviorstudio/termcade@latest"
				commandNote="Needs a truecolor terminal at 80×24 or larger"
				links={[{ href: 'https://github.com/aviorstudio/termcade', label: 'Source on GitHub' }]}
			>
				<p>
					Classic games rendered at sub-cell resolution with block pixels, so a terminal cell becomes
					a 2×2 or 2×3 grid of pixels and the playfield gets sharper than the font should allow. It
					ships with Asteroid and Tetris, playable offline the moment it starts.
				</p>
				<p>
					The arcade compiles no games into itself. Every game is a sandboxed WebAssembly package
					running under wazero with no filesystem and no network, so a broken one shows up dimmed on
					the menu with its reason rather than taking the arcade down. The same binary is the dev
					kit: <code>termcade dev new you/mygame</code> scaffolds one, <code>termcade dev build</code>
					packages it, and anyone can publish for players to <code>termcade add</code>.
				</p>
			</Entry>

			<Entry
				name="Castle Drop"
				tagline="Physics-first castle defense"
				status="Prototype"
				meta="Godot 4.4 · private repository"
			>
				<p>
					A castle defense where the physics is the game rather than the garnish. Clear a siege,
					draft a tower, place it on a rampart slot, then use that tower's abilities to amplify
					what you were already doing by hand — grabbing attackers, throwing them, and turning
					enemies into collisions with each other.
				</p>
				<p>
					Progression is castle upgrades only; there is no meta-currency layer bolted on beside it.
					Waves, enemies, towers and tuning are all authored as Godot resources in the Inspector,
					so the balance lives in the editor rather than in code.
				</p>
			</Entry>

			<Entry
				name="Prizm"
				tagline="Drag-and-drop light puzzle"
				status="Prototype"
				meta="Godot 4.4 · private repository"
			>
				<p>
					Spend limited colour mana to cast prism spells into empty drawer slots, then drag those
					prisms onto the board so beams from the transmitters bend into the receivers that need
					them. Rooms are authored as scenes in the editor, and the beam simulation resolves each
					board from the pieces you have placed.
				</p>
				<p>
					Deliberately mechanics-first: no avatar to move, no combat, no health, no timer and no
					fail state in the MVP. The whole puzzle is what light does when you put something in
					front of it.
				</p>
			</Entry>

			<Entry
				name="Fields of Revik"
				tagline="Tactics card game"
				status="In development"
				meta="Godot 4.4 · private repository · site live"
				links={[{ href: 'https://www.fieldsofrevik.com', label: 'fieldsofrevik.com' }]}
			>
				<p>
					A tactics card game on a coloured tile board, with animal factions — outlaw raccoons,
					monastic bears, and more — whose identity comes from their mana costs rather than a
					faction tag, so custom decks are first-class rather than a mode.
				</p>
				<p>
					Offline play against the machine and online play against a person run the same rules: the
					deterministic gameplay lives in one shared core used by both the client and a headless
					Godot server, and online matches are fully server-authoritative. The client never gets to
					decide what happened.
				</p>
			</Entry>
		</section>

		<section class="aside-note">
			<p>
				The three Godot projects run on the studio's own devtools — GDAM pins the addons they share,
				gdlint checks the GDScript.
			</p>
			<a class="ghost-cta" href="/devtools">See the devtools <span class="icon" set:html={icon('arrow-right', 13)} /></a>
		</section>
	</main>
</Full>

<style>
	.page-hero { width: min(1180px, calc(100% - 40px)); margin: 60px auto 70px; }
	.eyebrow { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 9px; margin: 0 0 22px; color: var(--quiet); font: 620 11px/1 var(--mono); letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
	.eyebrow span { width: 7px; height: 7px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--bone); box-shadow: 0 0 0 4px rgba(247, 242, 233, .1); }
	/* Narrow enough that the line breaks where the sentence does, between the
	   roman half and the italic one, instead of mid-phrase. */
	h1 { max-width: 640px; margin: 0; color: var(--bone); font-size: clamp(42px, 5.4vw, 68px); font-weight: 590; line-height: .99; letter-spacing: -.058em; }
	h1 em { color: var(--quiet); font-family: Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif; font-weight: 400; }
	.lede { max-width: 620px; margin: 28px 0 0; color: var(--quiet); font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; }

	.entries { width: min(1180px, calc(100% - 40px)); margin: 0 auto 90px; }

	.aside-note { width: min(1180px, calc(100% - 40px)); margin: 0 auto 40px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 40px; border-radius: 10px; background: var(--raised); border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 34px 40px; }
	.aside-note p { max-width: 640px; margin: 0; color: var(--quiet); font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.7; }
	.ghost-cta { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 7px; flex: none; color: var(--paper); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 6px 0; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 650; text-decoration: none; }
	.ghost-cta:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--paper); }

	@media (max-width: 980px) {
		.aside-note { align-items: flex-start; flex-direction: column; gap: 22px; }
	}
	@media (max-width: 600px) {
		.page-hero, .entries, .aside-note { width: min(100% - 28px, 1180px); }
		.page-hero { margin: 36px auto 50px; }
		h1 { font-size: clamp(36px, 11.5vw, 52px); }
		.lede { margin-top: 22px; font-size: 15px; }
		.aside-note { padding: 26px 24px; }
		.aside-note p { font-size: 13.5px; }
	}
</style>
